---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeRadixText from '@/components/tools/dev/DevEncodeRadixText.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="octal-to-text">
  <DevEncodeRadixText client:load radix="octal" direction="decode" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Base 8, three bits at a time</h2>
    <p>
      Octal uses the digits 0–7, and each digit is exactly three bits. That is why it survived: machines
      with 12, 24 and 36-bit words split evenly into octal digits, and hex (four bits per digit) did
      not. When 8-bit bytes became standard, hex was the natural fit, and octal remained in only a few
      areas.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Character</th><th>Octal</th><th>Decimal</th><th>Hex</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>A</code></td><td>101</td><td>65</td><td>41</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>a</code></td><td>141</td><td>97</td><td>61</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>060</td><td>48</td><td>30</td></tr>
          <tr><td>space</td><td>040</td><td>32</td><td>20</td></tr>
          <tr><td>line feed</td><td>012</td><td>10</td><td>0A</td></tr>
          <tr><td>maximum byte</td><td>377</td><td>255</td><td>FF</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Three digits per byte</h2>
    <p>
      A byte needs three octal digits, because 377 is the largest value. So for octal to text, input
      without spaces must be a multiple of three characters: <code>101 145 154</code> is unambiguous,
      and <code>101145154</code> parses only if each value was padded. A length error is probably an
      unpadded small value: <code>12</code> where you meant <code>012</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Where octal still appears</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Escape sequences: <code>\101</code> in C, Python and many regex flavours is the character
        <code>A</code>. Perl, sed and printf all read the same form.
      </li>
      <li>
        File permissions: <code>chmod 755</code> is octal, but it encodes permission bits, not
        characters. Use the chmod calculator for that.
      </li>
      <li>
        Legacy protocols and hardware: PDP-11 lineage documentation, some industrial controllers, and
        a few network standards use only octal.
      </li>
      <li>
        Unix tools: <code>od -c</code> and <code>od -b</code> still print octal by default, which
        surprises people who expect hex.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The leading-zero trap</h2>
    <p>
      In old C, a leading zero meant octal, so <code>010</code> was 8, not 10. This caused so many bugs
      that modern languages removed it: Python 3 rejects leading zeros without <code>0o</code>, and
      JavaScript treats them as an error in strict mode. Now the explicit <code>0o</code> prefix is the
      correct form. This tool accepts it on input and can add it on output.
    </p>

    <h2>Converting octal by hand</h2>
    <p>
      The easiest way is through binary. Write each octal digit as three bits, join them, then group
      them into bytes again:
    </p>
    <pre><code>110 145 154 154 157
= 001001000 001100101 001101100 001101100 001101111
regrouped into 8-bit bytes:
= 01001000 01100101 01101100 01101100 01101111
= 72 101 108 108 111
= "Hello"</code></pre>
    <p>Going the other way, take the bits three at a time from the right.</p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
